</sidebyside>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-substitution-examples">
<title>Choosing a Transformation</title>

<p>
In practice we usually run the process in the other direction: the
region of integration <m>R</m> is awkward in <m>xy</m>-coordinates, and
we look for a substitution under which it corresponds to a simple
region<mdash/>ideally a rectangle<mdash/>with constant limits of
integration. The boundary curves of <m>R</m> usually tell us what
<m>u</m> and <m>v</m> should be.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-parallelogram-substitution">
<title>A Parallelogram Region</title>
<statement>
<p>
Use the transformation <m>T(u,v) = (u - 2v,\, v)</m> to compute
</p>

<me>
\iint_D (x + 3y)\, dx\, dy,
</me>

<p>
where <m>D</m> is the parallelogram bounded by the lines
<m>x + 2y = 6</m>, <m>x + 2y = 10</m>, <m>y = 1</m>, and <m>y = 3</m>,
shown in <xref ref="fig-parallelogram-sub"/>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,14);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,13.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the example A Parallelogram Region.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-parallelogram-sub-video">
<caption>
The substitution straightens the parallelogram <m>D</m> into a
rectangle. Watch what happens to areas, and compare with the Jacobian
you computed.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-parallelogram-sub"
       source="videos/parallelogram_sub_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-parallelogram-sub">
<caption>
The parallelogram <m>D</m> between the lines <m>x + 2y = 6</m>,
<m>x + 2y = 10</m>, <m>y = 1</m>, and <m>y = 3</m> (left), and the
corresponding rectangle <m>R</m> in the <m>uv</m>-plane (right).
</caption>
<sidebyside widths="48% 44%" valign="middle">
<image>
<shortdescription>
A parallelogram between two slanted lines and two horizontal lines.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-parallelogram-D">
<diagram dimensions="(300,240)" margins="10">
<definition>l1(x) = (6 - x)/2</definition>
<definition>l2(x) = (10 - x)/2</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1,-0.8,11.5,5.8)">
<grid-axes at="pd-grid" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<polygon closed="yes" at="pd-region" points="((4,1),(8,1),(4,3),(0,3))"
         fill="#e9dcae" stroke="none"/>
<graph at="pd-line1" function="l1" domain="(-0.8,11)" stroke="#d02090"
       thickness="2"/>
<graph at="pd-line2" function="l2" domain="(-0.8,11)" stroke="#d02090"
       thickness="2"/>
<line at="pd-y1" p1="(-0.5,1)" p2="(11,1)" stroke="gray" dash="4 3"
      thickness="1"/>
<line at="pd-y3" p1="(-0.5,3)" p2="(11,3)" stroke="gray" dash="4 3"
      thickness="1"/>
<label at="pd-l2-label" anchor="(6.4,3.3)" alignment="ne"
       color="#d02090">
<m>x+2y=10</m>
</label>
<label at="pd-l1-label" anchor="(0.15,0.85)" alignment="se"
       color="#d02090">
<m>x+2y=6</m>
</label>
<label at="pd-D" anchor="(4,2)" alignment="c">
<m>D</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Two parallel lines with slope negative one half, x plus 2 y equals 6 and x plus 2 y equals 10, drawn in pink. Between them, the horizontal lines y equals 1 and y equals 3 cut off a parallelogram D, shaded tan.">
  <annotation ref="pd-region" text="The parallelogram D, bounded by the two pink lines and the horizontal lines y equals 1 and y equals 3."/>
  <annotation ref="pd-line1" text="The line x plus 2 y equals 6."/>
  <annotation ref="pd-line2" text="The line x plus 2 y equals 10."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
<image>
<shortdescription>
The rectangle R in the u v plane, image of the parallelogram.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-parallelogram-R">
<diagram dimensions="(300,240)" margins="(30,10,10,10)">
<coordinates bbox="(4.4,-0.8,11.5,5.8)">
<grid-axes at="pr-grid" xlabel="u" ylabel="v"/>
<polygon closed="yes" at="pr-rect" points="((6,1),(10,1),(10,3),(6,3))"
         fill="#e9dcae" stroke="#c07020" thickness="2"/>
<label at="pr-R" anchor="(8,2)" alignment="c">
<m>R</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The rectangle R in the u v plane, from u equals 6 to u equals 10 and v equals 1 to v equals 3, shaded tan. It is the image of the parallelogram D under the substitution.">
  <annotation ref="pr-rect" text="The rectangle R, with constant limits 6 to 10 in u and 1 to 3 in v."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-curved-to-rectangle">
<title>A Curved Region Mapped to a Rectangle</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>D</m> be the region in the first quadrant bounded by the
hyperbolas <m>x^2 - y^2 = -3</m>, <m>x^2 - y^2 = 3</m>, <m>xy = 1</m>,
and <m>xy = 4</m>, shown in <xref ref="fig-curved-region"/>.
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find a transformation <m>T</m> that relates <m>D</m> to a rectangle
<m>R</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Compute the integral <m>\displaystyle \iint_D xy\, (x^2 + y^2)\, dx\, dy</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,14);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,13.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the example A Curved Region Mapped to a Rectangle.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-curved-region-video">
<caption>
The substitution maps the region between the four hyperbolas onto a
rectangle. Watch what the Jacobian does to the integrand.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-curved-region-sub"
       source="videos/curved_region_sub_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-jacobian-area-manim">
<caption>
The Jacobian as a local ratio of areas, animated. The lines
<m>u = \text{const}</m> and <m>v = \text{const}</m> cut <m>R</m> (left)
into small rectangles with sides <m>\Delta u</m> and <m>\Delta v</m>, and
<m>T</m> carries each one to a cell of the curved grid of <m>D</m>
(right, drawn enlarged). For the highlighted cell, with corner
<m>(u,v)</m>, the violet parallelogram,
spanned by <m>T_u\,\Delta u</m> and <m>T_v\,\Delta v</m>, where
<m>T_u = \left\langle \partial x/\partial u,\ \partial y/\partial u \right\rangle</m>
and
<m>T_v = \left\langle \partial x/\partial v,\ \partial y/\partial v \right\rangle</m>,
approximates its image. As the grid is refined, the area of the image
divided by <m>\Delta u\,\Delta v</m> settles on <m>|J|</m> at the corner:
check it against the Jacobian you found.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-jacobian-area-manim"
             iframe="jacobian-area-manim.html"
             width="100%" aspect="4:3">
<instructions>
<p>
Click or drag in either plane to pick a cell, or use the <m>u</m> and
<m>v</m> sliders; the <q>rectangle</q> slider makes the grid coarser or
finer, and the rectangle is always one of its cells. The card divides the area
of the patch by <m>\Delta u\,\Delta v</m>. <q>Zoom to patch</q>
follows the cell and its image in, so that both stay in view as the
grid is refined; <q>Parallelogram</q> and <q>Grid</q>
switch those layers off and on, <q>Play</q> runs a guided tour, and
<q>Full screen</q> fills the screen with the figure.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-curved-region">
<caption>
The region <m>D</m> bounded by the hyperbolas <m>x^2 - y^2 = \pm 3</m>
and <m>xy = 1</m>, <m>xy = 4</m> (left) corresponds to a rectangle
<m>R</m> in the <m>uv</m>-plane (right).
</caption>
<sidebyside widths="46% 46%" valign="middle">
<image>
<shortdescription>
A curved region bounded by two families of hyperbolas in the first quadrant.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-curved-D">
<diagram dimensions="(368,300)" margins="10">
<definition>hyp(x,y) = x^2 - y^2</definition>
<definition>prod(x,y) = x*y</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(0,0,3.8,3.1)">
<axes at="cd-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<polygon closed="yes" at="cd-region" points="((0.550,1.817),(0.586,1.707),(0.628,1.593),(0.678,1.475),(0.739,1.353),(0.812,1.231),(0.899,1.112),(1.000,1.000),(1.112,0.899),(1.231,0.812),(1.353,0.739),(1.475,0.678),(1.593,0.628),(1.707,0.586),(1.817,0.550),(1.862,0.684),(1.911,0.809),(1.964,0.926),(2.018,1.036),(2.074,1.140),(2.129,1.238),(2.185,1.332),(2.240,1.420),(2.295,1.505),(2.349,1.587),(2.402,1.665),(2.342,1.708),(2.283,1.752),(2.224,1.798),(2.167,1.846),(2.110,1.896),(2.054,1.947),(2.000,2.000),(1.947,2.054),(1.896,2.110),(1.846,2.167),(1.798,2.224),(1.752,2.283),(1.708,2.342),(1.665,2.402),(1.587,2.349),(1.505,2.295),(1.420,2.240),(1.332,2.185),(1.238,2.129),(1.140,2.074),(1.036,2.018),(0.926,1.964),(0.809,1.911),(0.684,1.862))"
         fill="#e9dcae" stroke="none"/>
<implicit-curve at="cd-hyp-neg" function="hyp" k="-3" stroke="#d02090"
                thickness="2"/>
<implicit-curve at="cd-hyp-pos" function="hyp" k="3" stroke="#d02090"
                thickness="2"/>
<implicit-curve at="cd-prod-1" function="prod" k="1" stroke="#2b6cb0"
                thickness="2"/>
<implicit-curve at="cd-prod-4" function="prod" k="4" stroke="#2b6cb0"
                thickness="2"/>
<label at="cd-hyp-neg-label" anchor="(2.32,2.62)" alignment="e"
       color="#d02090">
<m>x^2-y^2=-3</m>
</label>
<label at="cd-hyp-pos-label" anchor="(1.95,0.3)" alignment="e"
       color="#d02090">
<m>x^2-y^2=3</m>
</label>
<label at="cd-prod-4-label" anchor="(3.05,1.42)" alignment="ne"
       color="#2b6cb0">
<m>xy=4</m>
</label>
<label at="cd-prod-1-label" anchor="(0.35,0.55)" alignment="se"
       color="#2b6cb0">
<m>xy=1</m>
</label>
<label at="cd-D" anchor="(1.5,1.5)" alignment="c">
<m>D</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Four curves in the first quadrant: the hyperbolas x squared minus y squared equals negative 3 and positive 3, in pink, and the hyperbolas x y equals 1 and x y equals 4, in blue. They bound a curved four-sided region D, shaded tan.">
  <annotation ref="cd-region" text="The curved region D bounded by the four hyperbolas."/>
  <annotation ref="cd-hyp-neg" text="The hyperbola x squared minus y squared equals negative 3."/>
  <annotation ref="cd-hyp-pos" text="The hyperbola x squared minus y squared equals 3."/>
  <annotation ref="cd-prod-1" text="The hyperbola x y equals 1."/>
  <annotation ref="cd-prod-4" text="The hyperbola x y equals 4."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
<image>
<shortdescription>
The rectangle R in the u v plane, image of the curved region.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-curved-R">
<diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="10">
<coordinates bbox="(-4.3,-0.8,4.3,5.2)">
<grid at="cr-grid"/>
<polygon closed="yes" at="cr-rect" points="((-3,1),(3,1),(3,4),(-3,4))"
         fill="#e9dcae" stroke="#c07020" thickness="2"/>
<axes at="cr-axes" xlabel="u" ylabel="v" clear-background="yes"/>
<label at="cr-R" anchor="(1.5,2.5)" alignment="c">
<m>R</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The rectangle R in the u v plane from u equals negative 3 to 3 and v equals 1 to 4, shaded tan. It is the image of the curved region D under the substitution.">
  <annotation ref="cr-rect" text="The rectangle R with constant limits, negative 3 to 3 in u and 1 to 4 in v."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-substitution-3d">
<title>Substitution in Triple Integrals</title>

<example xml:id="example-parallelepiped-volume">
<title>Volume of a Parallelepiped by Substitution</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the volume of the parallelepiped shown in
<xref ref="fig-parallelepiped-tikz"/> by writing the equations of all
faces of the parallelepiped and describing it by three inequalities.
Use a suitable change of variables such that all the integral limits
are constants (as opposed to functions).
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,17);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,16.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the example Volume of a Parallelepiped by Substitution.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<remark xml:id="remark-box-product">
<p>
Finally, we would like to mention that there is a much easier way of
calculating the volume of a parallelepiped (using the so-called box
product); however, the method above is a rich example in calculus and
definitely worth learning. For example, you may need to integrate a
function over the parallelepiped, which cannot be done using the box
product.
</p>
</remark>

<figure xml:id="fig-parallelepiped-video">
<caption>
A change of variables shears the parallelepiped into a box. Compare
the box, and the volume, with what you found.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-parallelepiped"
       source="videos/parallelepiped_volume_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-parallelepiped-tikz">
<caption>
The parallelepiped of <xref ref="example-parallelepiped-volume"/>. Its
three edges at the origin end at <m>(1,0,0)</m>, <m>(0,3,0)</m> and
<m>(0,1,1)</m>.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-parallelepiped-tikz" width="80%">
<shortdescription>
A long slanted box in space with one corner at the origin.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A parallelepiped drawn with crimson edges in a three-dimensional
coordinate system. One corner is at the origin, and the three edges
that meet there end at the points <m>(1,0,0)</m> on the <m>x</m>-axis,
<m>(0,3,0)</m> on the <m>y</m>-axis, and <m>(0,1,1)</m>, so that the
top face is the bottom face raised one unit and shifted one unit in
<m>y</m>.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// The parallelepiped of the example: one corner at the origin, and the
// three edges there ending at (1,0,0), (0,3,0) and (0,1,1).  (Labelled by
// its corners: the bounding planes are what the student finds.)
import graph3;

size(13.5cm);
// Same viewpoint as the pgfplots figure (view={115}{18}).
currentprojection = orthographic((0.8619, 0.4019, 0.309));
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

pen oicrimson = rgb(165/255, 30/255, 55/255);

// the eight corners: bottom face z = 0, top face z = 1 shifted one unit in y
triple A = (0, 0, 0), B = (1, 0, 0), C = (1, 3, 0), D = (0, 3, 0);
triple Up = (0, 1, 1);
triple A1 = A + Up, B1 = B + Up, C1 = C + Up, D1 = D + Up;

// faces (translucent)
// the two slanted end faces a little darker
material facepen = material(0.25*oicrimson + 0.75*white + opacity(0.25),
                            emissivepen=0.25*oicrimson, specularpen=gray(0.05));
material endpen = material(0.4*oicrimson + 0.6*white + opacity(0.45),
                           emissivepen=0.3*oicrimson, specularpen=gray(0.05));
draw(surface(A--B--C--D--cycle), facepen);
draw(surface(A1--B1--C1--D1--cycle), facepen);
draw(surface(A--D--D1--A1--cycle), facepen);
draw(surface(B--C--C1--B1--cycle), facepen);
draw(surface(A--B--B1--A1--cycle), endpen);
draw(surface(D--C--C1--D1--cycle), endpen);

// edges
pen edgepen = oicrimson + linewidth(1.3pt);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle, edgepen);
draw(A1--B1--C1--D1--cycle, edgepen);
draw(A--A1, edgepen); draw(B--B1, edgepen);
draw(C--C1, edgepen); draw(D--D1, edgepen);

// axes
pen axispen = gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.7pt);
draw(Label("$x$", EndPoint, fontsize(17pt)), (-0.6, 0, 0)--(1.9, 0, 0), axispen,
     Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$y$", EndPoint, fontsize(17pt)), O--(0, 4.9, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$z$", EndPoint, fontsize(17pt)), O--(0, 0, 1.9), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));

// the corner at the origin and the ends of the three edges there
dot(A, linewidth(4pt)); dot(B, linewidth(4pt));
dot(D, linewidth(4pt)); dot(A1, linewidth(4pt));
label("$O$", A, NW, fontsize(15pt));
label("$(1,0,0)$", B, SE, fontsize(15pt));
label("$(0,3,0)$", D, SE, fontsize(15pt));
label("$(0,1,1)$", A1, NW, fontsize(15pt));
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-ellipsoid-volume">
<title>Volume of an Ellipsoid</title>
<statement>
<p>
Use the method of substitution in multiple integrals to show that the
volume of the ellipsoid
</p>

<me>
\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \frac{y^2}{b^2} + \frac{z^2}{c^2} = 1
</me>

<p>
is equal to <m>\dfrac{4\pi}{3}\, abc</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,8.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the example Volume of an Ellipsoid.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-ellipsoid-video">
<caption>
Stretching the unit sphere by factors <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m>
along the coordinate axes produces the ellipsoid; the Jacobian
<m>abc</m> scales the volume from <m>\tfrac{4\pi}{3}</m> to
<m>\tfrac{4\pi}{3}abc</m>.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-ellipsoid"
       source="videos/ellipsoid_volume_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-ellipsoid-tikz">
<caption>
A transformation <m>T</m> carries the unit ball <m>S</m> onto the solid
ellipsoid <m>E</m> with semi-axes <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, <m>c</m>.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-ellipsoid-tikz" width="97%">
<shortdescription>
A unit sphere on the left is mapped by an arrow labeled T to an
ellipsoid on the right.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
On the left, the unit sphere <m>u^2 + v^2 + w^2 = 1</m>, labeled
<m>S</m>, drawn as a translucent gray-blue ball. An arrow labeled <m>T</m> points to the right, where the ellipsoid
<m>x^2/a^2 + y^2/b^2 + z^2/c^2 = 1</m>, labeled <m>E</m>, is drawn as
a translucent pink surface, wider than it is tall, with its three
semi-axes <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> drawn in vermillion, blue,
and orange.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// A transformation T carries the unit ball S onto the solid ellipsoid E with semi-axes a, b, c (here a = 2.4, b = 1.55,
// c = 1.05).  One scene: S at the origin and E beside it, to the right
// as seen from the viewpoint.
import graph3;
usepackage("amsmath");

size(16cm);
// Same viewpoint as the pgfplots figure (view={115}{18}).
currentprojection = orthographic((0.8619, 0.4019, 0.309));
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

// Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
pen oiblue       = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oivermillion = rgb(213/255, 94/255, 0/255);
pen oiorange     = rgb(230/255, 159/255, 0/255);
pen oipurple     = rgb(204/255, 121/255, 167/255);

triple right = (-0.4226, 0.9063, 0);   // to the right on the page
real a = 2.4, b = 1.55, c = 1.05;
triple C = 4.6*right;                  // center of E

// param: (polar angle, azimuth), so that the normals point outward
triple ballpt(pair t) { return (sin(t.x)*cos(t.y), sin(t.x)*sin(t.y), cos(t.x)); }
triple ellpt(pair t) {
  return C + (a*sin(t.x)*cos(t.y), b*sin(t.x)*sin(t.y), c*cos(t.x));
}

// the unit ball S
draw(surface(ballpt, (0, 0), (pi, 2pi), 8, 16, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(0.5*oiblue + 0.5*white + opacity(0.55),
                         emissivepen=0.3*oiblue, specularpen=gray(0.05)));
draw(circle(O, 1), gray(0.6) + linewidth(0.4pt));

// the ellipsoid E
draw(surface(ellpt, (0, 0), (pi, 2pi), 8, 16, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(0.45*oipurple + 0.55*white + opacity(0.25),
                         emissivepen=0.4*oipurple, specularpen=gray(0.05)));
draw(shift(C)*scale(a, b, 1)*circle(O, 1), gray(0.6) + linewidth(0.4pt));
// (E is kept faint, and is reddish purple rather than orange, so that the
// semi-axes seen through it keep their colors: blue seen through orange
// turns green)

// semi-axes
draw(C--C + (a, 0, 0), oivermillion + linewidth(1.4pt));
draw(C--C + (0, b, 0), oiblue + linewidth(1.4pt));
draw(C--C + (0, 0, c), 0.8*oiorange + linewidth(1.4pt));
label("$a$", C + (0.6a, 0, 0), S, oivermillion + fontsize(15pt));
label("$b$", C + (0, 0.65b, 0), S, oiblue + fontsize(15pt));
label("$c$", C + (0, 0, 0.7c), W, 0.75*oiorange + fontsize(15pt));

// the arrow T between them
draw(1.3*right--2.75*right, gray(0.25) + linewidth(1pt), Arrow3(size=9));
label("$T$", 2.025*right, 1.5N, fontsize(15pt));

// equations and names
label("$u^2+v^2+w^2=1$", (0, 0, 1.15), N, fontsize(15pt));
label("$\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}+\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=1$", C + (0, 0, 1.3), N,
      fontsize(15pt));
label("$S$", -1.15*right + (0, 0, -0.85), fontsize(17pt));
label("$E$", C - 2.0*right + (0, 0, -0.8), fontsize(17pt));
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-jacobian-activity">
<title>Activity: Watching a Substitution Transform a Region</title>

<activity xml:id="activity-jacobian-geogebra">
<title>From a Rectangle to a Curved Region</title>
<statement>
<p>
Use the transformation
</p>

<me>
x = \frac{u^2}{4}, \qquad y = \frac{uv}{4}
</me>

<p>
to compute
</p>

<me>
\iint_E \frac{x}{y^2}\, dx\, dy,
</me>

<p>
where
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
E = \Bigl\{ (x,y) \in \R^2 : \amp 1 \le x \le 4,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \tfrac{1}{2}\sqrt{x} \le y \le \tfrac{5}{2}\sqrt{x} \Bigr\}.
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
Also, make sure to graph the region of integration in the
<m>uv</m>-plane. <em>Note: choose a suitable substitution such that the
limits of integration are numbers as opposed to functions.</em>
</p>

<p>
The interactive below shows exactly this transformation at work: a
rectangle in the <m>uv</m>-plane (left) is carried by
<m>T(u,v) = \left( \tfrac{u^2}{4}, \tfrac{uv}{4} \right)</m> onto a
curved region in the <m>xy</m>-plane (right). Drag the rectangle's
corner and adjust its dimensions until its image is exactly the region
<m>E</m>, and use what you see to read off the constant
<m>uv</m>-limits.
</p>

  <interactive geogebra="bvcz6sbs"
             width="100%" aspect="11:10"
             material-width="618" material-height="559">
<instructions>
<p>
Use the <q>Dimensions</q> sliders to change its length and width; the
shaded region in the <m>xy</m>-plane is its image under
<m>x = u^2/4</m>, <m>y = uv/4</m>. Press <q>Animation</q> to watch the
rectangle sweep out its image, and <q>Clear Traces</q> to start over.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside widths="60% 32%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (9,12);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,11.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the activity.</shortdescription>
</image>
<image>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7, >=stealth]
\draw[gray!30, very thin] (-0.5,-0.5) grid (5.5,5.5);
\draw[->, thick] (-0.5,0) -- (5.9,0) node[right] {$u$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-0.5) -- (0,5.9) node[above] {$v$};
\foreach \x in {1,2,3,4,5} \draw (\x,0.12) -- (\x,-0.12) node[below=1pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {1,2,3,4,5} \draw (0.12,\y) -- (-0.12,\y) node[left=1pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
<shortdescription>Empty u v grid running from zero to five in each direction, for sketching the region of integration in the u v plane.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>

</activity>


</subsection>

</section>
